  "worked_solution": "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=\u0394\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=\\sqrt{0}+0+C\\,\u27f9\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+t+2.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=\\sqrt{1}+1+2=4\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=\\sqrt{4}+4+2=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\u0394\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =192-48 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
